    "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function",
    "problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then what is $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1?$",
    "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1=5\\sqrt{2}-1.\n$$",
